steps to run this file on your machine 
Step 1: Create a Deployment YAML file
Step 2: Apply the YAML
kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml

Expected output:

deployment.apps/nginx-deployment created
-------------------------------------------
Step 3: Verify the Deployment
kubectl get deployments

Example:

NAME               READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
nginx-deployment   1/1     1            1           10s
------------------------------------------------
Step 4: Verify the Pods
kubectl get pods

Example:

NAME                                READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
nginx-deployment-594cc45b78-pq5dx   1/1     Running   0          20s
---------------------------------------------------
Step 5: Describe the Deployment
kubectl describe deployment nginx-deployment
------------------------------------------------------
Step 6: Describe the Pod

First list the Pods:

kubectl get pods
--------------------------------------------------------
Step 7: View the Logs
kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl logs nginx-deployment-594cc45b78-pq5dx

-------------------------------------------------
Step 8: Modify the YAML

Open the file:
vim nginx-deployment.yaml

For example, change:
replicas: 1
to
replicas: 3

Save and exit.
-----------------------------------------------
Step 9: Apply the Updated YAML
kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml
-------------------------------------------------
Step 10: Update the Image

Edit the YAML:
image: nginx:latest

Change it to:
image: nginx:1.27

Apply it again:
kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml

Kubernetes performs a rolling update, replacing Pods one by one without downtime.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 11: Delete the Deployment
kubectl delete -f nginx-deployment.yaml

Output:

deployment.apps "nginx-deployment" deleted